---
// BigFix case study, built from the Figma frames "BigFix" (1680), "BigFix / 1280",
// "BigFix / 1024" and "BigFix / Mobile 320". Follows the My HCLSoftware template.
// Image pairs sit side by side down to 1024 and stack on phones.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Link from '../components/Link.astro';
import CaseFigure from '../components/case-study/CaseFigure.astro';
import Kpis from '../components/case-study/Kpis.astro';
import { url } from '../utils/url';
import '../styles/case-study.css';
import hero from '../assets/bigfix/hero.png';
import before from '../assets/bigfix/before.png';
import now from '../assets/bigfix/now.png';
import stackedChart from '../assets/bigfix/stacked-chart.svg?raw';
import chartDataTable from '../assets/bigfix/chart-data-table.svg?raw';
import bluePalette from '../assets/bigfix/blue-palette.svg?raw';
import colourBlindnessTests from '../assets/bigfix/colour-blindness-tests.svg?raw';
import navigation from '../assets/bigfix/navigation.svg?raw';
import tableDensities from '../assets/bigfix/table-densities.svg?raw';
import handoffLabel from '../assets/bigfix/handoff-alternative-label.svg?raw';
import handoffHeading from '../assets/bigfix/handoff-heading.svg?raw';

const results = [
  { figure: '100+', description: 'screens delivered.' },
  { figure: '6+ products', description: 'unified, and counting: on-prem, SaaS, IVR, DEX, MCM and SSA.' },
  { figure: '10 designers', description: 'led across Italy and India.' },
  { figure: '1 architecture', description: 'from grids to charts.' },
  { figure: '20+', description: 'layout patterns guarantee consistency.' },
  { figure: 'Accessible', description: 'by design.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="BigFix case study"
  description="How I lead the BigFix UI: one accessible architecture for 6+ products, a chart system built around colour blindness, and a handoff framework the team has made its own."
>
  <article class="case-study">
    <div class="hero">
      <h1 class="hero__title">BigFix case study</h1>
      <CaseFigure
        src={hero}
        alt="The BigFix Fixlet Explorer overview on a laptop, with quick links and two charts."
        width={716}
        minHeight={465}
        eager
      />
    </div>

    <Kpis label="Results" items={results} />

    <div class="summary">
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          Some of the people who use BigFix love it enough to have its logo tattooed on their skin. That is the trust I was asked to carry into a new interface
          when Aaron Bauer, who owns the project to rebuild the BigFix UI, asked for me by name, because he wanted to be sure it succeeded. The brief was to bring
          every BigFix product into one coherent, accessible, modern and attractive interface, one that finally looked as good as the product was trusted to be,
          without giving that fiercely loyal base any reason to feel the product had stopped being theirs.
        </p>
      </div>

      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="role">
        <h2 id="role">My role, created for me</h2>
        <div class="text">
          <p>
            I own the UI of BigFix. I am the final decision-maker on how every screen looks and behaves, in a role that did not exist until it was created for me,
            and together with Aaron and the UX lead I steer the whole effort, working with stakeholders and leadership across the United States, India and Italy
            through several meetings every week.
          </p>
          <p>
            I started from the grids and built upwards: the patterns every page is made of, the empty and error states, the architecture that six products now
            stand on. The design language I had already built for MHS and Marketplace gave me the foundation, and BigFix is where it grew into a much larger
            system, one that is now becoming Mandala, a fully AI-readable and compatible design system with a case study of its own.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="team">
        <h2 id="team">Leading the team</h2>
        <div class="text">
          <p>
            I lead a high-performing team of ten designers, distributed between Italy and India. I took part in hiring some of them and mentor all of them, as well
            as four external designers currently collaborating with us, and I run team meetings every week so that the team always has the context, the trust and
            the room it needs to do its best work.
          </p>
          <p>
            My own role has moved from hands-on design to review and final sign-off, and I share more of that review with the team every month, so that the
            quality of BigFix never depends on a single person. I keep the final sign-off; the judgement behind it is increasingly theirs.
          </p>
          <p>
            The best part of it is that it is a team people are glad to belong to. They work hard, they trust each other, and they have the freedom to follow the
            work that interests them most. Some of what I am proudest of in this case study, like the Figma plugins they built to speed up handoff, came from them,
            not from me.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" aria-labelledby="mandate">
        <h2 id="mandate">The mandate</h2>
        <div class="text">
          <p>
            BigFix is not one product. Long before it belonged to HCL it was a name IT professionals trusted, and the people who use it every day notice every
            change, because they live inside it for hours at a time. That made the stakes high in both directions: every change had to feel familiar to the people
            who already relied on it, and the new interface also had to become a real asset for the sales team, because in a competitive market the first thing a
            prospective customer judges is what the product looks like. It was a brief with very little room for a wrong answer.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="architecture">
      <h2 id="architecture">The architecture</h2>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          Building one architecture for six products meant that everything a page is made of had to share the same logic, not only the same look: the layouts, the
          components, the behaviours, the empty and error states and the charts. The foundation I had built for My HCLSoftware and Marketplace was a starting
          point, not a template. BigFix needed a new primary colour, a vastly larger component library and an entirely new chart library, because it runs on data
          in a way those earlier products never did.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="figure-stack">
        <div class="section">
          <h3>Before: one product, many different looks</h3>
          <CaseFigure
            src={before}
            alt="Four screens from BigFix products before the redesign, each with a different look."
            width={1230}
            minHeight={0}
          />
        </div>
        <div class="section">
          <h3>Now: a modern, consistent look and feel, accessible by design</h3>
          <CaseFigure
            src={now}
            alt="Four screens from BigFix products after the redesign, sharing one look and feel."
            width={1230}
            minHeight={0}
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="charts">
      <h2 id="charts">The chart problem</h2>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          A chart with thirty data elements cannot have thirty accessible colours. Human vision does not support that many distinguishable hues, and colour
          blindness narrows the number further still. I built a six-colour palette within a single hue for the cases that need only that, and a separate
          eight-colour palette engineered to hold up under every major type of colour blindness while still meeting contrast requirements, and that was the
          ceiling.
        </p>
        <p>
          Rather than fight the ceiling, I built the system around it. Every chart displays its top elements directly and groups the rest under “other”, and every
          chart, without exception, ships with a paired data table in a drawer that is sortable, downloadable and exact. A chart can struggle when a value of one
          sits next to a value of thirteen hundred, but the table never does, so accessibility is guaranteed at the system level rather than earned chart by chart.
          The table also makes every chart easier to read for everyone, which is what I have argued in accessibility training for years: accessibility is rarely a
          trade-off against a better experience, and usually it is the better experience.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="figure-stack">
        <div class="image-pair">
          <CaseFigure
            svg={stackedChart}
            alt="A stacked bar chart in six shades of blue, with a legend."
            width={459}
            minHeight={420}
            caption="The stacked chart using the fully accessible blue colour palette."
          />
          <CaseFigure
            svg={chartDataTable}
            alt="A chart reference drawer showing the chart data as a table."
            width={560}
            minHeight={420}
            caption="All charts have a data table which can be opened in a drawer. This perfectly meets requirements for long alt text for charts, as well as being useful to a lot of other users. A great example of how everyone benefits from accessibility."
          />
        </div>
        <div class="image-pair">
          <CaseFigure svg={bluePalette} alt="Six shades of blue, from light to dark." width={80} minHeight={340} caption="The blue colour palette." />
          <CaseFigure
            svg={colourBlindnessTests}
            alt="The six shades of blue as seen with eight types of colour blindness, from protanomaly to achromatopsia."
            width={545}
            minHeight={340}
            caption="Tests performed to check for colour blindness compatibility. These tests were performed on all themes, and on all types of palettes."
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="density">
      <h2 id="density">Density, resolved by choice</h2>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          BigFix's long-time users want maximum information on screen, while modern conventions favour breathing room and scrolling. I chose not to treat that as a
          fight to win and built the choice into the system instead. The left navigation collapses to icons for people who want the space, with labels still
          guaranteed on hover so nothing is lost to accessibility, and every data table offers three row heights, so each user sets their own density.
        </p>
        <p>Nobody has to lose this argument, because the system holds both answers at once.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="image-pair">
        <CaseFigure
          svg={navigation}
          alt="The left navigation expanded with labels, and collapsed to icons only."
          width={329}
          minHeight={380}
          caption="Navigation available both with labels and as icon only."
        />
        <CaseFigure
          svg={tableDensities}
          alt="Three data tables side by side, each with a different row height."
          width={553}
          minHeight={380}
          caption="Data table available in three different densities."
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="filtering">
      <h2 id="filtering">Filtering, built with the people who know it best</h2>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          Filtering is one of the hardest interactions in enterprise software to get right, and the credit for solving it belongs to Aaron and the UX lead. They
          understood the product, its users and its technical limits well enough to define how filtering had to work. I joined at the end of that thinking,
          shaping how the pattern looks and fits into the system, which is the smallest part of the problem.
        </p>
        <p>
          It is also one of the parts of this project I learned the most from. Watching them work through it taught me more about how people use BigFix than any
          brief could, and my job was to listen closely and make their solution look as good as it works.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="handoff">
      <h2 id="handoff">The handoff framework</h2>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          Handoff is where good design most often gets lost in translation, so nothing leaves the BigFix design team without a framework that answers developers'
          questions before they have to ask them. It sets out how every screen behaves from large desktop to mobile, and every accessibility decision a designer
          controls: page regions, heading hierarchy, tab order and where it must be deliberately forced, labels on icon-only buttons, alt text, spacing tokens and
          correct component usage. The result is code that is right the first time, a clear standard for QA to test against, and a clean line of responsibility:
          design guarantees accessibility on its side, and development and QA own it from there.
        </p>
        <p>
          The framework has since become the team's own. When security restrictions ruled out off-the-shelf Figma plugins, the designers built their own to make
          handoff faster, and seeing them extend the system on their own initiative is one of the things I am proudest of as their lead.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="image-pair">
        <CaseFigure
          svg={handoffLabel}
          alt="A handoff label reading Documentation, pointing to a report icon."
          width={207}
          caption="Alternative labels for screen reader users are documented in each design handoff."
        />
        <CaseFigure
          svg={handoffHeading}
          alt="A handoff label reading h1, pointing to the text I am the main title."
          width={270}
          caption="The designers indicate heading hierarchy when handing off to development."
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section--padded" aria-labelledby="outcome">
      <h2 id="outcome">6+ products speaking the same language, to everyone</h2>
      <div class="text">
        <p>
          The measure of this work is not any single screen. It is that six products now speak the same language, and that the people who have trusted BigFix for
          years can open any of them and still feel it is theirs, with accessibility built into every pattern and component so that everyone can enjoy it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The designers who carry it forward understand it well enough to take it further than I could alone, and what began as the foundation for two portals is
          becoming something more ambitious still: Mandala.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section--padded" aria-labelledby="next">
      <h2 id="next">Next case study</h2>
      <div><Link href={url('/mandala/human-governed-ai-powered/')}>Mandala case study: Human-governed, AI-powered</Link></div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Shared layout is in src/styles/case-study.css; this is what only this page needs */

  /* The intro and the three headed sections sit 24 apart, as in Figma "Section: summary" */
  .summary {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space_24);
  }

  /* Groups of figures 24 apart: "Before and after", "Chart figures" */
  .figure-stack {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: var(--space_24);
  }
</style>
